The data source is a flat ADS-B event timeseries where each row describes an aircraft's state at a specific time. The distinct real-world entity types are: (1) the aircraft itself, uniquely identified by the icao24 column; (2) the observation event per timestamp and aircraft, identified by icao24 and time together; (3) the geographic point defined by lat and lon, mapping to an AbsolutePoint; (4) a Location wrapper around that point; (5) a Velocity entity built from velocity, heading, and vertrate; (6) an Orientation entity from heading; and (7) an Altitude entity from geoaltitude. The aircraft maps to bsm:Aircraft because icao24 is the standard ICAO 24-bit aircraft address. The observation maps to bsm:Observation, which carries observationDateTime from time and links via unattached object properties hasVelocity, hasOrientation, hasAltitude, and inherited hasGeographicLocation. AbsolutePoint uses bsm:latitudeDecimal and bsm:longitudeDecimal. Location uses bsm:hasGeometry to the point. Velocity uses bsm:speedMetersPerSecond, bsm:absoluteBearingAngle, bsm:inclinationAngle. Orientation uses bsm:headingAngle. Altitude uses bsm:altitudeMeters. Columns callsign, squawk, onground, and baroaltitude have no matching ontology property and remain unmapped. All subject expressions use only column placeholders and are repeated exactly in referencing properties. Confidence and evidence are assigned primarily from column names and structure.

